# FreeToken — MoE serving. The weights come from an autumn FUSE mount, but this
# image contains no autumn code at all.
#
# It used to. It lifted `autumn-fuse` / `autumnfs` / `autumn-op` out of an
# AUTUMN_IMAGE, and built autumn's PyO3 client from source with a full rustup +
# cargo toolchain for a kvcache adapter nothing loads. That made one tag bind
# two projects: every autumn change touching the mount cost a 20 GiB rebuild and
# a 12 minute pull, and rkyv's WIRE lockstep applied here through a build-arg no
# image tag can show.
#
# Now the pod's initContainer copies `autumn-fuse` out of the autumn-rs image at
# start. The mount binary is a DEPLOY-time choice -- a fuse change is a manifest
# edit -- and this image is free of the lockstep entirely.
#
#   docker build -f docker/Dockerfile.freetoken \
#     --build-arg BASE_REGISTRY=hub-cache-cn-beijing.cr.volces.com/ \
#     --build-arg PIP_INDEX_URL=https://mirrors.ivolces.com/pypi/simple/ \
#     -t <cr>/freetoken-autumn:<tag> .
#
# ── Why a -devel CUDA base and not a slim runtime ──────────────────────────────
# FreeToken JIT-compiles CUDA kernels on first use and `freetoken/kernel/
# _toolchain.py:check_nvcc_matches_torch()` REFUSES to build when nvcc's CUDA
# major differs from torch's. So nvcc must be on PATH at RUNTIME, not just at
# build time. docs/install.md states the floor plainly: "Linux x86_64, NVIDIA
# GPU, driver r580+ (CUDA 13)". The 4090 node (192.168.3.2) runs 580.105.08;
# the 4090D node (192.168.0.102) is on 550 and CANNOT run this image.
#
# ── WIRE lockstep ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This image embeds the autumn PyO3 client, so it is bound by the same rule as
# vllm-autumn and hermes-autumn: build it at the SAME commit as the cluster core.
# rkyv has no cross-version compatibility and the handshake refuses a mismatch
# (and, worse, a matching WIRE fingerprint across a layout drift decodes garbage
# silently). AUTUMN_IMAGE below should name that same commit's tag.

ARG BASE_REGISTRY=
# CUDA 13 + nvcc. Overridable because the exact patch tag moves; anything
# 13.x -devel with a glibc >= 2.28 userland works (FreeToken's CI wheels are
# manylinux_2_28, cp310-cp313; Ubuntu 22.04 ships cp310).
ARG CUDA_IMAGE=nvidia/cuda:13.0.1-devel-ubuntu22.04
# The autumn-rs image to lift `autumn-fuse` out of — MUST be the same commit.
# Deliberately NOT prefixed with BASE_REGISTRY: that knob exists to route the
# PUBLIC base images through a mirror, and this one is a full private-registry
# reference already (`<host>.cr.volces.com/<ns>/autumn-rs:<sha>`). Prefixing it
# would produce `hub-cache-cn-beijing.cr.volces.com/<host>.cr.volces.com/...` and fail to pull.

FROM ${BASE_REGISTRY}${CUDA_IMAGE}

# ── Mirror knobs (override for China / air-gapped builds) ─────────────────────
#   RUSTUP_DIST_SERVER / RUSTUP_UPDATE_ROOT : rustup TOOLCHAIN download mirror.
#   CARGO_MIRROR   : crates.io registry mirror for the wheel build.
#   PIP_INDEX_URL  : PyPI mirror. The DEFAULT index resolves pypi.org fine but
#     downloads wheels from files.pythonhosted.org, which is slow/blocked from
#     the CN build pool — a build sits with no output for many minutes.
#     Use mirrors.aliyun.com, NOT mirrors.ivolces.com: the ivolces PyPI mirror
#     does not carry `freetoken` at all (`from versions: none`, build #55), while
#     aliyun has the cp310-cp313 manylinux wheels.
#   APT_MIRROR     : Ubuntu apt mirror BASE URL (e.g. https://mirrors.aliyun.com).
ARG PIP_INDEX_URL=https://mirrors.aliyun.com/pypi/simple/
ARG APT_MIRROR=
# torch for CUDA 13 is not on PyPI proper; it comes from its own index, which is
# why this is a separate arg from PIP_INDEX_URL.
#
# Mirrored, and that is not optional here. The upstream index resolves fine but
# serves the wheels from files.pythonhosted.org, which from the CN build pool
# crawls: a build died after 549 s of ReadTimeoutError on exactly this step
# while every other pip call in this file was already mirrored. It is the same
# trap the PIP_INDEX_URL comment above describes, one step further down, reached
# because torch brings its own index and so skipped that protection.
#
# The mirror carries cu100 through cu132, cu130 included, with the
# torch-2.11.0+cu130 cp310 manylinux wheel this needs (checked 2026-09-04).
# Point the arg back at download.pytorch.org if the mirror ever lags a release.
ARG TORCH_INDEX_URL=https://mirrors.aliyun.com/pytorch-wheels/cu130

ENV PATH=/usr/local/cuda/bin:$PATH \
    RUSTUP_DIST_SERVER=${RUSTUP_DIST_SERVER} \
    RUSTUP_UPDATE_ROOT=${RUSTUP_UPDATE_ROOT} \
    PIP_INDEX_URL=${PIP_INDEX_URL} \
    DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive \
    PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1

RUN if [ -n "$APT_MIRROR" ]; then \
      find /etc/apt \( -name '*.list' -o -name '*.sources' \) -print | while read -r f; do \
        sed -i -E "s#https?://(archive\.ubuntu\.com|security\.ubuntu\.com|ports\.ubuntu\.com)#${APT_MIRROR}#g" "$f"; \
      done; \
    fi \
 && apt-get update \
 && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
      python3 python3-pip python3-dev python3-venv \
      curl wget ca-certificates git build-essential pkg-config libssl-dev \
      libfuse3-3 fuse3 numactl aria2 jq \
 && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* \

# ── oniond: Volcengine TOS model downloader ───────────────────────────────────
# Model weights come from the internal TOS bucket, not HuggingFace: it is
# in-network, needs no AK/SK, and pulls with aria2 in parallel — where hf-mirror
# is an internet round trip for every one of 44 shards.
# Installed the way lerobot-agent-console does: pull the .deb from the ivolces
# extra-tools mirror, extract just the two binaries (its aria2-byted dependency
# is skipped), and point aria2c_byted at the stock aria2c. amd64-only, which
# this CUDA base is.
#
# `jq` is a runtime dependency of the oniond shell driver, not of the .deb we
# extract from — without it every invocation dies with "jq is not installed"
# (found by running `oniond list model` in the built image).
ARG ONIOND_MIRROR=http://mirrors.ivolces.com/extra-tools/debian
ARG ONIOND_DEB=onion-ai-data_2.4.6_amd64.deb
RUN cd /tmp \
    && wget -q "${ONIOND_MIRROR}/pool/main/o/onion-ai-data/${ONIOND_DEB}" -O "${ONIOND_DEB}" \
    && dpkg-deb -x "${ONIOND_DEB}" /tmp/onion-extract \
    && cp /tmp/onion-extract/usr/bin/hfd /tmp/onion-extract/usr/bin/oniond /usr/local/bin/ \
    && chmod +x /usr/local/bin/hfd /usr/local/bin/oniond \
    && ln -sf /usr/bin/aria2c /usr/local/bin/aria2c_byted \
    && rm -rf /tmp/onion-extract "/tmp/${ONIOND_DEB}" \
    && oniond help >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
# Default bucket for `oniond list` / `oniond download` (override with -e BUCKET=).
ENV BUCKET=ai-infra

# `autumn-fuse` mounts the fs/ namespace so FreeToken can read weights as files.
# Lifted from the core image rather than rebuilt: one Rust build, one binary,
# and it cannot drift from the cluster it talks to.

# ── torch first, pinned to the CUDA-13 build ──────────────────────────────────
# FreeToken requires torch>=2.11,<2.12 and its extensions compile against it, so
# torch must be present and correct BEFORE freetoken is built/installed.
RUN python3 -m pip install --no-cache-dir --index-url ${TORCH_INDEX_URL} "torch>=2.11,<2.12"

# ── FreeToken ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A pip spec, so this can point at a released wheel or a git ref:
#   --build-arg FREETOKEN_SPEC='freetoken[accel]==<version>'
#   --build-arg FREETOKEN_SPEC='freetoken[accel] @ git+https://github.com/FlashML-org/FreeToken@<sha>'
# NOTE: `[accel]` pulls flashinfer + sglang-kernel. It does NOT pull vLLM, and
# that is deliberate — vLLM supplies the Marlin NVFP4 expert-GEMM path for
# sm_80-99 (which is what a 4090 needs for the fastest NVFP4 kernels), but it
# pins transformers>=4.56,<5 against FreeToken's own transformers>=5.5. Upstream
# calls this out in pyproject.toml and leaves it out of the resolution. Serve
# with `--nvfp4-backend triton` instead; revisit only if expert GEMM measures as
# the bottleneck, and then in a dedicated image.
# Pinned, not floating: an engine upgrade should be a deliberate edit here, not
# something that happens because a rebuild ran on a different day.
# 0.1.2 was checked against the source this deployment was designed from
# (git e05cff8, 18 commits ahead): moe/nvfp4_backends.py, moe/cpu_executor.py and
# kvcache/base.py are all present, and the CPU-MoE format whitelist
# `_WFMT_IDS = {bf16, nvfp4, mxfp4_triton, ds_fp4, q4_0}` is identical — which is
# the fact the model choice rests on (fp8 experts cannot run on the CPU side, so
# an FP8 checkpoint silently loses hybrid and falls back to pure offload).
ARG FREETOKEN_SPEC=freetoken[accel]==0.1.2
# Its own index, NOT PIP_INDEX_URL. Not every mirror carries this package: the
# ivolces PyPI mirror answers `from versions: none` for it (build #55 died
# there), and a caller who sets PIP_INDEX_URL for speed should not have to know
# that. Pinning the index here means the build works whatever the general mirror
# is, which is the difference between a Dockerfile that is correct and one that
# is correct only when someone remembers a pipeline parameter.
ARG FREETOKEN_INDEX_URL=https://mirrors.aliyun.com/pypi/simple/
RUN FREETOKEN_ALLOW_CUDA_MISMATCH=0 \
    python3 -m pip install --no-cache-dir \
      --index-url "${FREETOKEN_INDEX_URL}" "${FREETOKEN_SPEC}"

# ── the autumn client + its kvcache adapters ──────────────────────────────────
# The SOURCES come out of the autumn-rs image, alongside the binaries above.
#
# This used to `COPY . .` the build context, which made the wheel's wire version
# come from whatever commit that context was at while `autumn-fuse`/`autumnfs`
# were lifted from a separately pinned AUTUMN_IMAGE. Nothing forced the two to
# agree, and a wheel at one wire version with binaries at another is a build
# that looks fine and dies at the handshake — or decodes garbage silently if the
# fingerprint happens to match across a layout drift. It also meant this file
# could only be built with the autumn-rs repo as its context, which is not where
# it lives any more.
#
# Now AUTUMN_IMAGE is the ONE version knob and the build context is this repo.
#
# We take SOURCE and compile here rather than copying a prebuilt wheel, because
# the wheel is a native extension with no abi3: it is bound to the builder's
# glibc and exact CPython. autumn-rs builds on Debian bookworm (glibc 2.36);
# this image is Ubuntu 22.04 (glibc 2.35, Python 3.10), so a wheel from there
# would either be rejected by pip as manylinux_2_36 or install and fail at
# import on a missing GLIBC_2.36. Compiling against this image's own runtime
# avoids the whole question.
# NOTE: this image used to build autumn's PyO3 client here -- a full rustup +
# cargo toolchain and a maturin build of the workspace, ~1.5 GB of it -- for the
# kvcache adapter. Nothing loads that adapter: the manifest passes no
# --hicache-storage-backend, weights come from the mount, and the wheel was
# imported by no code that runs. It went, and with it the only reason this image
# had to be built at the cluster's commit.
#
# `autumn-fuse` is not here either. The pod's initContainer copies it out of the
# autumn-rs image at start, so the mount binary is a DEPLOY-time choice: a fuse
# change costs a manifest edit, not a 20 GiB rebuild and a 12 minute pull.


# `ft serve` defaults to 127.0.0.1:1919 and has NO authentication of any kind —
# no API key, no bearer. Expose it as a ClusterIP only, never a LoadBalancer.
# 1920 is also reserved (distributed init binds server_port + 1).
EXPOSE 1919
# No ENTRYPOINT: the pod supplies the full command, because it has to resolve the
# manager ClusterIP first (autumn's Rust client parses SocketAddr — IP literals
# only, no DNS) and start the FUSE mount before `ft serve` can read the weights.
CMD ["ft", "--help"]
